critiques.strongestPoint:
B is the only option here where, once migrated, neither device is ever refused the other's epr release for ISA reasons, in both directions, so definition-of-done line 1 can be met and stay met. It needs no host modelling to get there.

What I verified:
- **No fleet roll.** I ran graph-walker read-only on every path B touches (pool-policy.json, run-local-gate.sh, gate-preflight.mjs, a new orchestrator test, hc-mesh.sh, epr-cli sources). None dispatches elohim-edge; only elohim-orchestrator, elohim-eprfs and the two bridge pipelines fire.
- **The seconds check has a real home.** gate-preflight.mjs runs before any build in both pre-push and `just gate`.
- **Linked-in host objects are clean in the crashed binary.** It has no x86 ISA-needed note, and the support code at the end of its text (`__pthread_atfork`, `_init`, `_fini` and the 128-bit helpers, which are Rust's compiler_builtins and not RHEL's libgcc) contains no v3-only instruction. The startup stubs near `_start` were not disassembled. This is one binary, but it supports B's claim that a C-flag baseline is enough for epr today.
- **Tests will not trip on the new variable.** The branch's artifact tests clear or scrub the environment at every spawn site I checked.
- **Cargo itself ties the objects to the flag.** Because cc-rs tracks the variable, a slot's C objects are rebuilt whenever its value changes, which closes the "warm slot compiled under something else" class (survivor 7) without new code.

Worth grafting into whichever option wins:
- the constant set beside the Rust toolchain pin;
- env:isa-c as a set marker, read by value;
- the legacy v3 floor;
- deferring the device-runtime hook (the only piece that rolls the fleet) to its own lane;
- the ring DW_AT_producer string as a seconds-level bytes witness on the receipt.
